{"text":"Recent work supports testing both necessity and sufficiency of interaction subsequences. [FCESR (2025)](https://arxiv.org/abs/2504.13632) searches for minimal influential subsequences using reinforcement learning; [Demystifying Sequential Recommendations (2025)](https://arxiv.org/abs/2508.03606) uses genetic algorithms to find minimal history changes that alter recommendations. Neither prediction change alone nor a plausible explanation establishes useful temporal reasoning. [Refining Fidelity Metrics (SIGIR 2025)](https://sigir2025.dei.unipd.it/detailed-program/paper?paper=e21e4e58ad9ab56e8a4634046da90113) identifies problems with mixing supporting and contradictory interactions and comparing fixed-percentage deletions across histories; it advocates fixed explanation lengths.

Recency and accumulated preferences deserve separate controls. [Petrov et al. (RecSys 2026)](https://research.atspotify.com/2026/10/do-sequential-recommendation-benchmarks-require-higher-order-sequence-modelling) report that history-aware pairwise models outperform SASRec/eSASRec reproductions on four of five benchmarks, while earlier interactions improve over last-item controls. Thus, useful history dependence need not imply complex joint sequence reasoning. Their Amazon categories differ from the four target categories here, so transfer remains unverified.

Diagnostic implication: use chronological pseudo-holdouts drawn exclusively from training histories; compare equal-count recent removal, older removal, and repeated random removals. Shuffle retained items separately, including a control preserving the last item, to distinguish last-item dependence from broader order sensitivity. Keep candidate sets fixed; measure paired changes in target rank/HR@10 alongside top-k overlap and score shifts. These are proposed controls synthesized from the literature: accuracy degradation beyond matched random interventions suggests useful temporal dependence; large recommendation changes without corresponding accuracy effects suggest instability. Repeat across intervention sizes and report category-level uncertainty before linking failures to recency weighting or sequence encoding."}
